A||||32||Fformula88||Anyone with a lowered Fiero...||||1||
Z||000000||Fformula88||10-22-2001||08:09 AM||||I would like to hear your thoughts, opinions and see some pics on your lowered Fiero, sespecially any 88's!<P>The rear springs are shot on my Formula, and I am considering a set of lowering springs.   I am concerned about the lower ground clearance in regards to parking lots etc, but also for stress on the rest of the suspension.  Will this cause me some headaches in wear of other components?  I would be replacing all shocks/struts with some performance versions.<P>My other choice is to get some used springs.  My concern is after buying them, having them shipped in, and paying for their installation (I don't like to play with coil springs!) that they won't be any good either, or last 1 year and start saging themselves.  I have been everywhere... Napa, Advance, Auto Zone, Pep Boys, even Pontiac and nobody can get these things!  So my only choice for springs is used, or new lowering springs.<P>Also, whats everyone's opinion on the brands of lowering springs out there.  I know the Fiero Store has a set of custom made 88 lowering springs, and I think I saw another manufactureer (like Eibach or someone) offered them for the 88 also.  Opinions on these would also be great!<P>Thanks a lot everyone!!<P>------------------<BR>John  <BR>AOLIM: Fformula88<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/formulasig.JPG">         <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <P>1988 Fiero Formula<BR>1985 Fiero SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||10-22-2001||08:37 AM||||Although mine is not an 88, I have had mine lowered for about 2 years now. Using lowering springs will stiffen the suspension so you will feel all the bumps in the road. I haven't had any ground clearence problems during normal driving. I do avoid steep angled driveways and have had no problems with speed bumps. The pain is jacking the car up. I usually drive onto a piece of wood to get the car high enough to get the jack under it. A flat tire would be a huge pain.<BR>I store the car for winter so I've never had it in the snow since it was lowered. I prefer the stiff, tight handling and the lowered look of my car. I don't think I would do it to a daily driven car or at least not tighten up the suspension like mine is now.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fcol_01_1.jpg"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">          <BR><FONT SIZE="1"><B><I>Happiness isn't around the corner...<BR>Happiness IS the corner.</I></B></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Brian87egotrip||10-22-2001||09:02 AM||||I have an 87gt I lowered with the kit from the fiero store. I love it. no ground clearance problems. rides a little rougher but does handle better. mine is a daily driver. It's been done for about 3 years now I have not noticed any extra stress or wear and tear. Sorry no pics.<P>------------------<BR>Brian                    87GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroLisa||10-22-2001||09:21 AM||||I lowered my 86... Jacking up in a pain in the rear. I have to use the stock Fiero jack to get the floor jack under it.  Also took it to a muffler shop and they had a helluva time getting it on the lift...had to raise the rear of the lift, then place 8 foot boards on front to drive up for a less steep angle.  <BR>Driving over potholes makes me want to cry for my car, but otherwise I love the way they look so its just fine!! (pic in sig)<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/fierolisa.gif">     <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fierolisa/" TARGET=_blank>FieroLisa's Website</A>*<A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ncfieros/" TARGET=_blank>NC Tarheels Fieros Site</A><P>AIM: FieroLisa||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||10-22-2001||09:41 AM||||I have Eibach's on my Formula - does ride stiffer, no abnormal clearance problems yet. 57K miles on them so far||||reg||1
Z||000005||BlackStang95||10-22-2001||10:18 AM||||I think you'll be fine, the stock fiero has a decivingly high amount of ground clearance, and aftermarket shocks are almost always better than stock.  Go for it.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||AkursedX||10-22-2001||10:24 AM||||Go for the lowering springs.  It'll make the car look so much better, but the ride comfort will suffer.  My '88 has poly all around, KYB's all-around, and the lowering springs.  The handling is simply amazing, but driving around on Buffalo city streets is a little rough at times, but I think it is worth it.  <P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000007||bHooper||10-22-2001||10:48 AM||||i have the Eibachs, KYB's and poly bushings on my Gt.  It is stiff, but controlable.  I like the ride, and seldome have a problem with scraping. <P>on my SE, it has been lowered with drop spindles, and springs in the rear.  this car is totally different from the one i did myself... she scrapes everywhere, and realy tall speed bumps will scrape the center of the car.  also, the person that did the install removed the entire bump stop, and the front tires "top out" into the tire well.<P>i perfer the springs.  i have installed two sets (mine and Lisa's), and i can tell you it is nothing to be afraid of.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><FONT COLOR="red"><B>Red</B></FONT></FONT> 86 GT 5 speed<BR><FONT SIZE="3"><B>Black</B></FONT> <u>86 SE Automatic</u>||||reg||6
Z||000008||Joe Torma||10-22-2001||11:05 AM||||My car is an 86...my opinion...lower your car with decent springs!  Lowest point is 1 1/2 off the ground.  Its the roadkill catching rear anti sway bar.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||10-22-2001||12:42 PM||||Oreif: ...Your hood is sweet..How'd u do that?<P>As for lowering..no matter what anyone will tell you ..don't chop them. It changes the spring rate to be stiffer. <P><BR>Get a name brand..Can't go wrong with eibachs. I have had 2 sets now. <P>JM ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||10-22-2001||12:42 PM||||Orief, thanks for all the pics!  Your Fiero looks great!  The lowering is more subtle than I would have expected.  I can certainly tell there is less room under the wheel wells than stock, but not excessively less.  I like that a lot!<P>Akused, do you have the Eibach's on your GT?  Also, do you know how much the Eibach's lower the 88?  With all the favorable responses, it sounds likelowing springs is the best way to go!  Now I need to decide which springs to get.  Eibach or TFS.  I am guessing the Eibach's are probably a better spring since Eibach can afford to design a better spring than I would think TFS could.  I'd trust their spring knowledge first.  As a side note... those car swallowing pot holes in Buffalo can get bad!  My grandfather hit one on Main St. near Delavan last winter.  It caused the bumb stop on his 90 New Yorker to go right thru the metal plate it is supposed to stop at on the rear axle, and damaged the spring mount on the axle!  It messed up the whole rear suspension!!  So if your ever in that area, watch out!<p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||SLYGUY||10-22-2001||01:24 PM||||From the factory the 88s seem to be the highest in front. <BR>The previous owner of my 88GT lowered the front. He did it the stupid way. He heated the front springs. I'm gonna replace them all the way around with some Eibach lowering springs as soon as possible. <BR>The ride, at least in my experience is noticably stiffer and will bottom if you are not careful. I'll take that as an equal tradeoff to the 4x4 curbhopping stock appearance.<P>Sly<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ViewImage.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||10-22-2001||01:42 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Oreif: ...Your hood is sweet..How'd u do that?<P>JM </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>A forum member named Coyote did it. Basically he just cuts the shape adds an insert and glasses it all in. It is simular to Archie's "Warber" hood with some minor changes. He canted the sides more and cut the front edge to match the edge of the hood.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChopTop||10-22-2001||02:13 PM||||If you going to the trouble of putting lowering springs in it might be a good idea to replace your rubber bushings with polyurethane ones at the same time, just remember they will add to the car stiffness.  Welcome to the LowLife club.||||reg||1
Z||000014||mshill||10-22-2001||02:51 PM||||The biggest problem I have seen with lowered Fieros is that you have to be real careful in parking lots cause the nose section can get pretty messed up on curbs and  the parking blocks.  The nose of my lowered 86GT was completely destroyed when I got it.<P>I was thinking about making some sort of sonar device whose sender receiver would sit right at the bottom edge of the nose and have green, yellow and red indictor lights to tell you how close the nose is to the curb.  I am absolutely going to have to get one for my F50 project, cause the nose is fiberglass and I don't want to crack that stuff up on a high curb.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Fformula88||10-22-2001||05:51 PM||||Where did you all get your 88 Eibach's?  I have been searching for a source online and came up empty.  Eibach doesn't have them listed on their site anymore.  Please point me in the right direction!||||reg||1
Z||000016||Jake_Dragon||10-22-2001||07:11 PM||||My 85 GT was lowered in the front before I bought it. The springs are cut off on the bottom. I haven't drove it much yet but it feels ok. The back looks stock.<P><FONT COLOR="red"><B>1985 GT</B></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/gt.GIF">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Jake_Dragon (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||AkursedX||10-22-2001||07:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR>[B]<P>Akused, do you have the Eibach's on your GT?  <BR>B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I have the FieroStore springs.  To be honest, I'm not sure is Eibach makes any for the 88's.  At least I couldn't find any when I went spring hunting.  So I went to the Fiero Store's springs.  They lowered the car about 1" in the front and 7/8" in the rear.  It looks perfect now, and I really have no complaints with the springs.  I know some people have had their problems with TFS lately but I have bought stuff from them before and I will continue to buy from them. <P>As far as curb scraping, there is only one place where I scrape and that driveway is really steep.  And I used to scrape it when i was stock height if I went up or down it quickly.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||10-22-2001||07:33 PM||||Mine's not lowered...yet.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  All I know is that I've ridden in Oreifs car and it handles like a sled on rails!  The ride is definitely stiffer, but I didn't think it was unduely harsh.  (This was even in Chicago!)||||reg||1
Z||000019||BAM-BAM||10-22-2001||07:56 PM||||  Guess I'll join in. Mine is lowered with Eibach Por Kit Lowering springs. I love the ride and the ijncreased handling. I also have Poly bushings, KYB shocks and struts, Konig Monsoons 17x7 with Kumho Ecsta 712's. I love the whole effect and feel.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SB1_2.JPG"> <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SB2.JPG">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/SBday11.JPG">  <P><BR>Peace, B2 OUT!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>  <p>[This message has been edited by BAM-BAM (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000020||Fformula88||10-22-2001||08:58 PM||||bam-bam, I love the whole look of your car!  Those wheels compliment the rest of the car perfectly.  Very nicely done, and thanks for the pics!<P>Akursed, I only found TFS springs in my searching tonight.  I personally don't have anything against them either and love their service, and it looks like they are going to be my only choice for new springs.  <P>I even checked out Eibach's website... they do not list the 88 Fiero on it so I am doubting I will find any... although I could have sworn they were available.  I must have been thinking of the 84-87 set.<P>Thanks for all the info everyone!!||||reg||1
Z||000021||danny boy||10-22-2001||09:11 PM||||  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/shelby_wheels.jpg">  <P>here's how<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/brakes_and_coilovers_1.jpg">  <P>Held motorsports coilovers and Fierostore springs(discontinued) I tore holes in the floor on a fill tube at a gas station. Don't go this low.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by danny boy (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||TI_3VOM||10-22-2001||10:07 PM||||Fformula88,<BR>   Check out American Custom Engineering's website. (http://www.americancustomeng.com)<BR>As copied from their price list:<BR>1988 ACE-EIBACH 1 1/4" DROP SPRINGS  Regular Price $ 352.00           Overstock Sale $ 252.00<P>...seems like they got Eibach to make them a few sets. HHHmmmm........||||reg||4
Z||000023||Fformula88||10-23-2001||10:12 AM||||Thanks for the tip!  I'll check it out after I get home tonight!  ||||reg||1
Z||000024||Standard||10-23-2001||10:32 AM||||Mine's been lowered with Fierostore springs in front and RCC coilovers in rear.  The biggest problem I have is I scrape the air dam everytime I back out of my driveway... Aside from that, just gotta remember to be a little more careful driving around town.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/drivers_rear.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rear_suspension.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/front_sus.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig_pic_full.jpg">          <BR><FONT COLOR="red">1988 Quad 4 Coupe 5 speed</FONT><BR><FONT SIZE="1"><FONT COLOR="white">1986 GT 5 speed, 1984 SC Auto</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">AOL: LastMinuteBastrd  AIM: SavedbyJebus</FONT></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Return of Fiero||10-23-2001||01:28 PM||||someone has posted something like this im sure, but, i had one of the 88 twins (those who know me know of these two cars) the upgraded one, was not lowered, it was dropped. on a flat surface, the lowest point on the car was 3/4 inch off the ground. serious problems jacking it up. had to have it put on a ramp truck once.it took 2 hours to get it on the truck. (and i paid thru da' nose!) and on a final note, i once got it stuck on a gas cap thingy (you know where the stations check the gas level, and get the tanks filled? red white yellow and blue caps?)spun like a m___________ F________ but didnt move an inch.<P>One the bright side, the car cornered alittle too good. 30 mph right hand turns with the lightest squeak out of the tires, no lean, no give on the rear. very sharp looking (mine were both notchbacks and they looked fine) <P>want my opinion? do it, just not as low as mine. mine was too low, and i paid too much for it to be that low.<P>Myke||||reg||14
Z||000026||sqoach||10-23-2001||01:51 PM||||Pre-88s<BR>Anyone have the ST's from JCWhitney? How much do they lower front and rear? I'm just considering those because they're about $100 cheaper than Eibach's. I'm looking for something to lower front and rear about 1 inch. I don't like the wheel well gaps, and I don't want to have to get tall tires to fill them up.<P>------------------<BR>-Adam<BR><FONT COLOR="BLUE">sqoach@yahoo.com</FONT><BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.gif">             <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">              <BR>^^^^^<B>new project</B>^^^^^<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><LI>86 SC 2.5L 5spd</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Elvira||10-23-2001||02:12 PM||||If you want to talk LOW Fiero, Chester's 86GT is so low (let's hear it: How low is it???) That we scrape the ground quite a bit to the point that he actually has to raise it a little.  If you put your hand outside the door (either one) and touch the ground, the bottom of the rocker panel is still on finger area below the palm (about 2-2 1/2 inches).  Now that's LOW!!  Alot of fun to drive though!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Elvira<BR>Blue 87GT<BR>Black 86GT 5sp x 2<BR>86SC t-top 58k miles||||reg||1
Z||000028||Joe Torma||10-23-2001||02:40 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by sqoach:<BR><B>Pre-88s<BR>Anyone have the ST's from JCWhitney? How much do they lower front and rear? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I got them...it came down at least an 1 1/2 front and back...more like 2.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||Tigger||10-23-2001||02:48 PM||||Sqoach, I have the rear ST springs.  I was in a hurry (like a kid with a new toy) and forgot to measure the rear height before tearing everything apart.  I'd say it dropped the rear of mine 1-1/4" probably with a V-6 it would be more like full 1-1/2" (Wished it dropped it 2" like Joe said)<P>They don't seem to be much stiffer than the stock springs but that's ok, should be just right after adding poly.<p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Dragon||10-23-2001||10:44 PM||||Lowering is better but rougher.<P>I used Suspension Techniques from JC Whitney to drop the "Dragon".  At the time I was using Monroe Struts and shocks an the ride was quite rough.  Then one day I was in the local auto parts recycle yards and happened upon a doomed 86 GT that happened to have Koni adjustable shocks.  I could not believe the difference a high quality shock makes.<BR> <A HREF="http://floridafieros.org/daytona2002/" TARGET=_blank>http://floridafieros.org/daytona2002/</A> <BR><P>------------------<BR>Dragon<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://SpaceCoastFieros.com/dragon.jpg">  <BR>   Space Coast Fieros||||reg||1
Z||000031||Spiff||10-24-2001||12:46 AM||||I just installed lowering springs, KYB shocks, and Poly bushings on my 88 Formula yesterday. I have not driven it yet so I can't give any real answer.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000032||sqoach||10-24-2001||11:43 AM||||2 inches, eh? Hmmm...that's a little lower than I'm wanting. If the roads didn't suck around here, I would dropped this thing to the ground. Plus our driveway is narrow and kinda steep. I'll have to think about this...||||reg||1
